# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 0 — MANDATORY: Agent Handoff Documentation
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- **READ FIRST**: At the start of EVERY session, read `docs/AGENT-HANDOFF.md`. It is the single source of truth.
- **WRITE LAST**: At the END of every session, UPDATE `docs/AGENT-HANDOFF.md` with:
  - What you built (flip ⬜ to ✅, add new entries)
  - What broke or was discovered (add to "Known Tech Debt")
  - What the next agent should do (update "NEXT" section)
- **NEVER** create other plan/context files (no WORKING-CONTEXT.md, no AI_HANDOFF_PLAN.md, no progress.txt, etc.)
- This project uses MULTIPLE AI agents. If you don't document your work, the next agent will redo or break it.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 1 — ECC: Context Persistence & Token Efficiency
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Before any implementation, re-read the active TODO list and the last completed task to avoid duplicate work.
- Do NOT re-read files you already read in the same session unless the content may have changed.
- Keep responses concise. Avoid verbose explanations unless the user explicitly asks for them.
- After completing a milestone, propose a logical stopping point before continuing to the next feature.
- Never speculatively implement features not in the current TODO item.
- Prefer targeted edits (multi_edit / edit tools) over full file rewrites.
- When exploring unfamiliar code, use code_search first, then grep_search. Do not read entire files blindly.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 2 — ARCHITECTURE: Domain-Driven Design (Vext-inspired)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Rust src-tauri/src/commands/ contains ONLY thin Tauri command handlers.
  - Commands must: validate input, call a domain function or DB query, return Result<T, String>.
  - Business logic (calculations, rules, aggregations) must NOT live inside #[tauri::command] functions.
  - If business logic exceeds ~20 lines inside a command, extract it to a private fn in the same file or a dedicated module.

- Rust src-tauri/src/db/ is the ONLY layer allowed to write raw SQL.
  - No SQL strings are allowed in commands/ files beyond parameterised queries directly on `conn`.
  - migrations.rs uses ensure_column() for additive migrations only — never DROP or ALTER TYPE.

- Frontend src/api/index.ts is the ONLY layer that calls Tauri `invoke()`.
  - React components must never call `invoke()` directly.
  - React components must never import from `@tauri-apps/api` directly.

- Frontend src/pages/ contains page-level orchestration only.
  - Reusable UI pieces belong in src/components/.
  - Data-fetching hooks belong in src/hooks/.
  - All Tauri/API calls go through src/api/index.ts.

- Types live exclusively in src/types/index.ts.
  - Do not define inline interfaces inside component files for shared data shapes.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 3 — DESIGN: Forest & Ivory Theme Guard (cursor-designer)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Theme Palette (enforce, do not deviate)
- Primary surface:   bg-ivory-50 / bg-ivory-100
- Primary brand:     bg-forest-600 / text-forest-600 / border-forest-600
- Danger:            text-status-danger / bg-red-50
- Success:           text-status-success / bg-forest-50
- Text main:         text-ink-main
- Text muted:        text-ink-muted
- Border:            border-ivory-border

## Layout & Density Rules (RTL-first)
- ALL layout classes that have directional meaning MUST use Tailwind logical properties:
  - Use `ms-*` / `me-*` instead of `ml-*` / `mr-*`
  - Use `ps-*` / `pe-*` instead of `pl-*` / `pr-*`
  - Use `rounded-s-*` / `rounded-e-*` instead of `rounded-l-*` / `rounded-r-*`
  - Exception: icon positioning with `absolute left-*`/`right-*` inside RTL-aware wrappers is acceptable.
- All panels use `app-panel` CSS class for consistent shadow/border radius.
- Form inputs use the `inp` CSS class from the page-level const — do not duplicate input styles inline.
- Icon size for form field decorators: size={16}. For action buttons: size={18}. For page headers: size={20}.

## Component Patterns
- Destructive actions always require a confirmation step (modal or inline confirm button).
- Empty states must include: an icon, a title, and a call-to-action button.
- Loading states use the existing `<Button disabled>` with `t('common.loading')` — no custom spinners.
- Toast messages use the existing `<Toast>` component — no browser `alert()`.
- All user-visible strings must have a key in src/i18n/ar.json. No hardcoded Arabic or English strings in JSX.

## Accessibility
- Interactive elements that are not `<button>` or `<a>` must have `role` and `tabIndex` attributes.
- Form labels must always be associated with their input via `htmlFor` / `id` or wrapping `<label>`.

## POS Touch Targets
- All interactive elements in POS/thermal-printer workflows must have a minimum height of 44px.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 4 — SUDANESE MARKET: Monetary Display Standards
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- ALL monetary values are stored as integers (fils/piastres × 100). Display by dividing by 100.
- Use api.formatMoney() for ALL monetary display — never format money inline.
- Monetary display MUST use Western/Latin digits (0-9), NOT Arabic-Indic digits (٠-٩).
- Currency symbol is SDG (Sudanese Pound). Display as suffix, not prefix: e.g. "125.50 SDG".
- Decimal separator: period (.). Thousands separator: comma (,). Example: "1,250.00 SDG".
- Never hardcode "SDG" in JSX — use `t('common.currency')` or derive from tenant settings.
- Negative balances (debts, deficits) display in red (text-status-danger) with a minus prefix.
- Profit/positive values display in green (text-status-success).

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 5 — SAFETY CONSTRAINTS
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Never delete database columns or tables. Use ensure_column() for schema changes only.
- Never modify license validation logic in license_guard.rs.
- Never bypass feature flag checks (FLAG_* constants) in command functions.
- Never store plaintext passwords. The existing bcrypt flow must remain intact.
- The cloud sync enqueue calls after mutations (cloud_sync::enqueue_owner_refresh_request) must remain in all create/update/delete commands.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 6 — CODE QUALITY (ECC common + TypeScript rules)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## File & Function Limits
- Max file length: 800 lines. If a file exceeds this, split it into focused modules — do not ask, just split.
- Max function length: 50 lines. Extract helpers with clear names.
- Max nesting depth: 4 levels. Use early returns to flatten logic.

## Code Hygiene
- No commented-out dead code. Remove it entirely.
- No magic numbers. Use named constants (e.g. `const MAX_RETRY = 3`).
- No `console.log` in production code. Use proper error/logging paths.
- Prefer immutable updates (spread operator, Object.assign) over in-place mutation of objects and arrays.

## Error Handling
- Every catch block must either re-throw, log with context, or return a meaningful error. No empty catches.
- Never silently swallow errors. If an error is intentionally ignored, add a comment explaining why.
- Error messages shown to users must never leak stack traces, SQL, or internal paths.

## TypeScript Strictness
- No `any` type. Use `unknown` + type narrowing. If `any` is truly unavoidable, add a `// REASON:` comment.
- Exported functions must have explicit parameter and return types. Let TS infer locals only.
- Use `interface` for object shapes that may be extended. Use `type` for unions, intersections, and utility types.

## Git Commits
- Commit format: `<type>: <description>` — types: feat, fix, refactor, docs, test, chore, perf.

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAYER 7 — REACT DISCIPLINE (ECC frontend patterns)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Component Typing
- Define component props with a named interface (e.g. `ProductCardProps`). Never use inline object types for props.
- Do not use `React.FC`. Use plain function components with typed props destructuring.

## Performance
- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations derived from props/state.
- Use `useCallback` for functions passed as props to child components.
- Use `React.memo` for pure components that receive complex or frequently-changing parent props.
- Use `React.lazy` + `Suspense` for heavy page-level components (charts, reports).

## Composition
- Prefer `children` and slot patterns over deep prop-drilling configurations.
- Extract repeated UI patterns into shared components in src/components/ — do not duplicate JSX across pages.
